summary:
The design is careful and well evidenced. Most of its soundness machinery answers hazards the research verified: elaborating Lean runs arbitrary code, an assumption can be smuggled into a definition, autoImplicit turns typos into quantifiers, and a kernel bypass fools #print axioms. Judged against the user's literal request and stated priority, though, v1 is over-scoped. The user said "because I want to use this" (S9) and asked to hear when it is "good enough for me to start using it" (S52). Yet §10 of doc 03 defines that point as the whole v1 column: about 20 deliverables, 4 npm packages plus a Lean package, and 5 test layers, including a CLI, Lean-editor providers, DECISION records, an independent back-translation session, Instruct, two-phase runs and gate steps G0–G6. Several recommendations also depart from the user's spec with little need:
- a five-kind schema (DEFINITION is a ★ question; DECISION is applied as an unstarred default);
- renaming the user's `type:` to `kind:`, with a diagnostic that nags about the user's own syntax;
- a fourth "Instruct" position on their three-way switch;
- a mid-run statement sign-off plus a comparator freeze, instead of S31's single diff "when the LLM is done".

D-23 presents only the Agent SDK. R7 recommended the learnharness-style `claude -p` stream-json route, R6 said direct spawning "has fewer moving parts", and the user pointed at learnharness themselves.

Several departures should stay because they protect soundness a formal-methods user will care about:
- strict selection (it is the literal meaning of S28);
- the LLM never adds assumptions (S7);
- adding a node never calls the LLM, as in the walkthrough;
- the user's natural-language text and rationale stay byte-identical;
- autoImplicit=false;
- bwrap around every Lean run on agent-written code;
- the G0 lint, leanchecker (about 1 s) and the axiom audit;
- the layout sidecar, a single comment carrier for markup, and the "LSP: not now" answer.

Two should become options: two-phase review (as a "Formalize first" path, which the design already has) and DEFINITION (as a role computed from Lean rather than a new kind). The docstring mirror should default to ON, since it was the user's own suggestion. The rest can be deferred: the comparator freeze, the compiled extractor, DECISION records, back-translation, editor providers, the CLI, Instruct, multi-root support, Shiki, pragmatic-drag-and-drop, Vite, the edge budget, vscode-test/xvfb, three-way merge and git auto-commit.

I ran one experiment (scratchpad/review/redteam/axp, Lean 4.35.0, no Mathlib). A generated check file with no `import Lean` gives fully qualified `#print axioms` sets, a Prop-or-not probe per axiom via `type_of%`, and canonical pp.all statements, in about 0.44 s and 470 MB per run. That covers v1's needs without the 231 MB compiled extractor. The design's interpreted fallback is the RAM-heavy path: a comparable `import Lean` script measured 1.6 s and 1.6 GB. The simpler alternative below covers every sentence S1–S55 with roughly half the components, makes the Cynder walkthrough run end to end the first usable milestone, and is a strict subset of the main design in data format and trust model, so the extras can be added later without migration.

issues.problem:
The ★ decision on how Claude is slotted in leaves out the option the research itself recommended and that matches the user's reference project: drive the installed Claude Code CLI headlessly with `-p --input-format/--output-format stream-json`, as learnharness does. The SDK route brings:
- a proprietary ('All rights reserved'), ESM-only package loaded by dynamic import() from a CJS bundle;
- a second ~328 MB Claude Code binary pinned by the SDK, instead of the installed 2.1.314;
- the SDK 0.3.N ↔ CLI 2.1.N version pairing;
- install-from-folder only;
- a custom PreToolUse path-guard hook for confinement.

issues.suggested_fix:
Add the CLI option to D-23 with these trade-offs, and recommend a ClaudeCliBackend for v0.1 behind the same LlmBackend interface, keeping the SDK as a later backend. ClaudeCliBackend:
- finds the binary in this order: setting → PATH → ~/.local/bin → getExtension('anthropic.claude-code').extensionPath + '/resources/native-binary/claude';
- spawns `-p --input-format stream-json --output-format stream-json --verbose --model claude-opus-5-5 --effort high --system-prompt-file <tmp> --restricted --tools Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write --strict-mcp-config --mcp-config <loopback http + token> --permission-mode dontAsk --allowedTools …` with cwd = shadow and API-key and snap variables stripped;
- never passes --bare, enforces a minimum version via --version and fails closed.

In either route, replace the custom path-guard hook with `--restricted` (the SDK can pass it through extraArgs; verify this). Before building, run one tiny smoke prompt to confirm subscription auth under the stripped environment and the --restricted confinement. Ask the user first, because it uses a little quota.

alternative.summary:
The same user-facing behaviour for every sentence of prompt1.md, built from the smallest set of parts:
- one VS Code extension package;
- markup in the user's own syntax;
- a three-column webview with one SVG edge overlay;
- a deterministic gate built from Lean's own commands plus one generated check file;
- Claude driven through the user's installed Claude Code binary in headless stream-json mode, with a handful of telelean MCP tools (the learnharness pattern, ideas only, no AGPL code);
- one-shot runs that end in a single reviewable diff.

Assurance extras come as opt-in increments once the user has used the tool. In data format and trust model it is a strict subset of the main design, so the main design's features can be added later without migration.

alternative.keeps:
(1) Representation (S34–S41):
- `/- @@ telelean` blocks with `id`, `type: ASSUMPTION|DERIVATION|GOAL`, `natural_name` (verbatim), `rationale`, `premises` and `origin`;
- line-based key: value, not YAML; unknown keys preserved; optional `v1` marker; `kind:` accepted as an alias;
- a code-less node is a comment only, never sorry;
- a formalised ASSUMPTION is an `axiom`; a formalised but unproved node is `theorem … := by sorry`, or the user's S37 def-statement scheme if they choose it;
- docstring mirror ON for formal nodes;
- `.telelean/layout.json` sidecar for order;
- one `Model.lean` plus lakefile.toml (autoImplicit=false, toolchain pinned to v4.35.0, no Mathlib).

(2) Board (S2, S11–S16, S25–S26, S41):
- an editor-tab WebviewPanel with three independently scrolling DOM columns and one SVG overlay (full redraw per animation frame, off-screen endpoints clamped);
- blue aura plus a none/sorry/proved/broken glyph;
- click selects into a tray below; a chevron expands the card from NL to its Lean source;
- drag across columns changes kind, with D-17's rules; drag within a column reorders;
- vocabulary appears as a collapsed box-card group at the top of ASSUMPTIONS, its role computed from Lean.

(3) Prompt box (S4, S10, S18–S24): exactly A|D|G plus text plus a 'why?' field; Add writes a comment block and never calls the LLM. A conversation strip shows progress, question cards and the proposal summary, and takes free-text answers, feedback and requests.

(4) Run (S27–S33):
- one Claude session per button press, formalising and proving in one go;
- questions along the way through a non-blocking ask_user;
- a single final proposal: graph overlay, 'Raw Lean diff' (vscode.changes), and per-statement 'You said / Lean says' rows (pp.all toggle) that must be ticked before Accept;
- Accept applies an undoable WorkspaceEdit; Feedback continues the same session (--resume after a reload); Discard;
- 'Formalize only' for a statements-first flow;
- insufficiency produces ghost assumptions that the user adopts (D-6), never silent axioms.

(5) LLM slot (S9): the LlmBackend interface with two backends.
- ClaudeCliBackend: binary from setting → PATH → ~/.local/bin → the Claude Code extension's native binary; `-p` stream-json, `--restricted`, `--tools Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write`, `--strict-mcp-config`, `--permission-mode dontAsk`, an allowlist and a custom system prompt; cwd = shadow; API-key and snap variables stripped; never --bare; minimum-version check.
- FakeBackend: replays scripted transcripts for tests.
- telelean tools (check_lean, ask_user, propose_assumption, finish) are served from a 127.0.0.1 Streamable-HTTP MCP endpoint with a per-run bearer token.

(6) Soundness:
- a shadow copy, and every Lean run on agent code under the design's bwrap profile: no network, tmpfs $HOME, read-only ~/.elan, read-only lakefile and toolchain, memory cap, timeout, fail closed;
- G0 lint (same deny list) → G1 sandboxed lake build → G2 leanchecker → G3 audit from one generated Check.lean: `#print axioms` with pp.fullNames, `type_of%` Prop probes, pp.all `#check`;
- G3 enforces: proved ⇒ no sorryAx; axioms ⊆ selected ∪ vocabulary ∪ {propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound}; every Prop axiom bound to an ASSUMPTION block; unresolved names fail closed;
- G5: natural_name and rationale byte-identical, ids stable, no deletions, only G→D reclassification;
- flags: proof from zero assumptions, unused selected premises, bodied definitions used, previously accepted statements changed.

(7) Sync and scripts (S3, S42, S43):
- a file watcher re-parses on edits;
- a debounced, single-flight lake build plus Check.lean refreshes the real workspace;
- `.telelean/graph.json` (format_version 1) is written on every refresh for scripts;
- the LSP answer stays 'not now', and the core module is free of `vscode` imports.

(8) Engineering:
- one TypeScript package, esbuild for host and webview, React or vanilla (start from R3's prototype), native HTML5 drag and drop;
- tests: vitest, Lean 4.35 fixtures (every exploit rejected), a FakeBackend controller walkthrough of S18–S33, and one manual Claude run;
- install: Node 24 tarball, npm install, build, 'Developer: Install Extension from Location…'.

alternative.drops:
Deferred, not deleted:
- the two-phase sign-off checkpoint, and the leanexport/comparator freeze (replaced by the 'accepted statements unchanged' pp.all check);
- the independent back-translation session;
- the compiled telelean-extract and the lean/TeleleanTools package (replaced by the generated check file);
- DEFINITION and DECISION as markup kinds (replaced by a computed vocabulary role; questions and answers stay in session logs and are fed to later runs);
- the Instruct switch position (replaced by the conversation strip);
- Lean-editor providers and the TextMate injection;
- the CLI and its JSON Schema (replaced by graph.json);
- the multi-root picker;
- the Vocabulary/Main/Decisions/Inbox file split;
- the EdgeEngine budget, culling, junction dots and port-drawn arrows (arrows come from the button's premises and from Lean);
- Shiki, pragmatic-drag-and-drop, Vite, and zustand (optional);
- Playwright, vscode-test and xvfb, so D-31 disappears;
